#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192473 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "3f141210-8194-5346-9e2a-c47f98bb3443" :question/math-academy-id "e-10840" :question/problem "Compute $P\\left( X > 8 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n![](images/85/8507c337ea73575abbc2127507f2b0ae8440b3587bf0191fe7344b8be6dfb1b3.svg)" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a <    X <    b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(8<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-5x}]}_{8}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192474 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "d40a67bd-7d98-58fb-b4a1-7a556e91556a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10851" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n![](images/48/48acd7d6ad8f581ba6033e19282fca0e43019c06ec2812c0698e50edd81075d6.svg)" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3}$ on the interval $x \\geq 0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3} \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(4x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{[\\frac{k}{8(4x+1)^{2}}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{8} = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad k = 8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "5f988175-cdd9-5ddf-94b5-3b995dc75fce" :question/math-academy-id "e-12329" :question/problem "![](images/15/1562da3da10e26ab9b4aff6adaa71fcbb1c2de963b4bfc682663f7fcfa62f8d6.svg)\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\geq 3$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{\\infty} f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x \\geq 3,$ we have $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(x-2)^2} \\geq 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }(x-2)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,{|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a01ed84-7ed4-5f6f-a73e-9bd30af3fabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423065} {:answer/id #uuid "7acfc4ce-813f-5b26-908f-36ee952d848e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423069} {:answer/id #uuid "81adb032-9089-5587-9426-36f061380efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423071} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe28e04-3646-5d84-8159-6ca62c717e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423073} {:answer/id #uuid "d012b452-3404-5413-b1e0-608d83d5420b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a01ed84-7ed4-5f6f-a73e-9bd30af3fabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423065} :answer-field/id #uuid "aad6b045-a52a-569f-a198-e336ad216b28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5cfff2a4-1c49-516b-a57f-2fc4f75675f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126017" :question/problem "Compute $P(X>6)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =P(6<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{6}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06a255f8-64ef-51d8-a6e6-a071190bf006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423059} {:answer/id #uuid "23d2d105-cdbc-500b-881f-d9e0d158fa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423061} {:answer/id #uuid "28421387-3618-5282-a14b-84e4566ab27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423062} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc57884-296c-5280-8d50-e3fcc9ff86ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423075} {:answer/id #uuid "fc72619e-93b0-55af-ae1f-db797a4bfb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc72619e-93b0-55af-ae1f-db797a4bfb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423076} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac73eb15-075e-5981-aef0-815786fcaa0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3576b4c9-6bad-5c07-bd64-6e08d7444e14" :question/math-academy-id "q-126021" :question/problem "Compute $P(X\\le -4)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\, & x\\le -1, \\\\ 0,\\, & x>-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -4) & =P(-\\infty <X\\le -4) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}_{-\\infty }^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a4da9c-5ad8-5dda-8ba1-be02f0af04f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423060} {:answer/id #uuid "5d964b39-2482-5346-8612-8ce043b95788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423066} {:answer/id #uuid "793d1056-34ae-50be-bbb1-25d45ceebdd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423068} {:answer/id #uuid "80eb078c-af7c-5800-a93c-6a0513121c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423070} {:answer/id #uuid "84482c4e-d104-578b-9bca-d5349bf9e6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d964b39-2482-5346-8612-8ce043b95788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423066} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e8275a-bb12-5f98-ba18-184bdee245ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7af6cdaa-d1ff-562c-9d47-7e9a37adf5ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-160446" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 0,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0019d257-76eb-58eb-993a-0411c1c8ea5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423057} {:answer/id #uuid "0345689f-fc2f-5460-9f38-e059246fb16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423058} {:answer/id #uuid "4c86aada-f7d7-57c4-bae2-63a127ef9b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423063} {:answer/id #uuid "56eca2a0-0c43-50ef-b63f-0ce7a632d9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423064} {:answer/id #uuid "76159088-6817-5983-996b-f9485affcb33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76159088-6817-5983-996b-f9485affcb33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423067} :answer-field/id #uuid "857db1a9-5baa-547f-ad7c-ffed08bafef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d743af0-a7a6-59cf-8a8b-38734a18e7f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-160458" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3x-1},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)=3{e}^{3x-1}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{e}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x-1}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56481835-518b-5171-88da-86e05963ba2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276245} {:answer/id #uuid "6273fab4-4c1d-5d8f-a0e5-081c7a5bdc04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276368} {:answer/id #uuid "71638daa-0052-5754-b71c-02dc8f380acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276498} {:answer/id #uuid "992c7b81-3eb7-55dc-bc8c-3bd914085869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276852} {:answer/id #uuid "a0222e17-38e9-574c-9fce-ece43f8640e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0222e17-38e9-574c-9fce-ece43f8640e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276920} :answer-field/id #uuid "9950b528-93fa-5fe7-a6ea-a3f27b30d432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4ee7fd8-9373-59cf-bc78-d62531a54190" :question/math-academy-id "q-160463" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}},\\, & x\\ge -2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}$ on the interval $x\\ge -2,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{k}{6(x+3)^{6}}\\,{|}_{-2}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{6}=1\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118c3392-8f4b-5a28-b170-f891ea364930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275633} {:answer/id #uuid "929258e4-1ed5-5b7d-8880-aba10bed2194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276804} {:answer/id #uuid "e0656531-5f92-50c3-8174-7a4c5b980686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277436} {:answer/id #uuid "e255bf4d-f521-5b32-8e26-405c65c4a7ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277458} {:answer/id #uuid "f106b996-8990-5fef-94a8-8ae99ae28da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "929258e4-1ed5-5b7d-8880-aba10bed2194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276804} :answer-field/id #uuid "afb72719-6e3d-599c-8a5a-a7d44b4323e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "008eb439-04f8-52ac-8507-0ab3780bebdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-160467" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{3x+2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{3x+2}$ on the interval $x\\le 0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}k{e}^{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{k}{3}{e}^{3x+2}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0}=\\frac{k{e}^{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k{e}^{2}}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{3}{{e}^{2}}.\n$$"}]}
